Now need to define `handleWorldTap`. Add after the pointerup listener or later. We'll add near input. It uses raycaster; define `const tapRaycaster = new THREE.Raycaster(); const tapPointer = new THREE.Vector2();` maybe at camera area. We can define right there.

Implementation:
```
function handleWorldTap(clientX, clientY) {
 const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
 tapPointer.x = ((clientX-rect.left)/rect.width)*2-1;
 tapPointer.y = -((clientY-rect.top)/rect.height)*2+1;
 tapRaycaster.setFromCamera(tapPointer, camera);
 const candidates = [];
 for (const item of groundItems) {
   if (item.carried || item.popping>0) continue;
   const dx=..., dz=...
   if (dx*dx+dz*dz > (PICKUP_RANGE+0.5)^2) continue;
   candidates.push(item.mesh);
 }
 if (!candidates.length) return;
 const hits = tapRaycaster.intersectObjects(candidates, true);
 if (!hits.length) return;
 let obj = hits[0].object; let item = null;
 while (obj) { if (obj.userData.itemRef) { item = obj.userData.itemRef; break; } obj=obj.parent; }
 if (item) pickUpItem(item);
}
```
Need `canvas` rect; use renderer.domElement or window. Good.
- If the tap hits a decoration bush (not item) it won't be in candidates.
- If the item is behind a tree, raycaster against only items might pick through tree. Not a big deal.
- If an item is near but off-screen, no.

Add after the pointerup listener.

